meta {
  name: 05 Write on an outbound route - EXPECT 403
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{gateway}}/payera/outbound/Patient
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{tokA}}
  Accept: application/fhir+json
  Content-Type: application/fhir+json
}

body:json {
  {
    "resourceType": "Patient",
    "name": [{ "family": "ShouldNeverLand" }]
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 403
}

docs {
  Assertion 7. The correct payer, the correct credential, the wrong verb.

  Layer 3 holds a per-route method allow-list. Outbound is read-only by
  definition: GET and the Group export, nothing else. There is no configuration
  in which this POST succeeds without editing the policy.

  This is the direct answer to Platform Engineering's question from 8/12 -
  whether one FHIR service can safely serve inbound and outbound at once.
  The route is not a suggestion about which door to use. It is the only door
  that exists, and each door runs a different allow-list before it opens.
}
